        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters in the DC market",
        "market_text": "<p>Washington turns over constantly — administrations, postings and contracts move people in and out on fixed timelines, which makes DC buyers decisive and photo-driven. A rowhouse in Capitol Hill or a condo in Navy Yard gets judged in a commute-length scroll; home staging is what stops the thumb.</p><p>The stock splits between historic rowhouses with formal, narrow rooms and new glass condos with open plans that photograph empty as bare concrete. Both need furniture to make the floor plan legible — one to modernize the traditional, the other to humanize the new.</p><p>DC's relocation clock is the practical argument. A buyer arriving for a fixed-term posting is house-hunting inside a few weeks and often from another city entirely, which means the listing has to be photograph-ready the day it goes live rather than a week later once a stager has been scheduled.</p>",
